That’s a particularly powerful connection. Because you’re pointing toward something deeper than semiotics in art:

What if physics, chemistry, biology, music, color, and language are different grammars for organizing relationships—and we could translate between them by identifying which structures survive?

Not because they’re literally the same thing, but because some of their organizing principles recur across domains.

And your guitar observation introduces another crucial idea: mode.

A mode changes which relationships are emphasized, how a system behaves, and what the same underlying material means within a different frame of reference.

1. Physics to chemistry to biology: when interactions acquire meaning

Physics

Energy · forces · fields · quantum states

Chemistry

Atoms · bonds · reactions · molecular structure

Biology

Receptors · signaling · regulation · adaptation

Cognition

Recognition · memory · attention · prediction

Culture & art

Symbols · conventions · metaphor · beauty

A hierarchy of descriptive and interpretive levels, not a claim that each layer reduces without loss to the next.

At the physics level, particles and fields interact according to physical laws.

At the chemistry level, those physical interactions support stable molecular structures and chemical reactions.

We describe the resulting regularities through chemical concepts such as bonding, valence, functional groups, reaction mechanisms, and energy landscapes.

The underlying physics hasn’t disappeared. We’ve changed descriptive scale and introduced useful higher-level variables. For instance, the Born–Oppenheimer approximation separates electronic and nuclear motion to make molecular calculations tractable.

Then biology introduces something especially interesting.

A molecule can become a signal.

Consider a hormone binding to a receptor.

Molecule

Receptor

Response

A binding event can initiate intracellular signaling cascades that regulate cellular behavior.

The molecule’s chemistry makes the interaction possible.

But the cellular system determines what functional consequence that interaction has.

Different cells can respond differently to the same molecule because they express different receptors and downstream signaling machinery.

This is an example of what we can call context-dependent biological interpretation.

It’s also where biosemiotics becomes relevant: studying living systems through sign relationships, signaling, and interpretation.

The distinction worth preserving is that a chemical reaction does not automatically possess meaning in the linguistic sense. A signal becomes functionally meaningful within an organized system capable of responding to it.

The recurring architecture is:

\boxed{\text{Pattern}+\text{Receiver}+\text{Context}\rightarrow\text{Response}}

That gives us a useful bridge among molecular signaling, sensory perception, computer protocols, and cultural communication—without confusing their distinct mechanisms.

2. Your seven layers and the seven musical modes

There’s a wonderful structural analogy here, although the numbers matching is coincidental.

My earlier seven artistic layers were analytical dimensions. The seven common diatonic modes are actual musical structures.

The modes offer a much more concrete demonstration of how changing the reference frame changes the experience.

Take the seven notes:

C – D – E – F – G – A – B

Use only those notes, but change which one functions as the tonal center.

Rotate the musical mode

